WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.789
How do you film something that is missing?

00:00:02.789 --> 00:00:04.984
Look at the opening in this mountain.

00:00:04.984 --> 00:00:09.688
Weâ€™ll give the crater a human scale, using one walker and one continuous camera move.

00:00:10.488 --> 00:00:12.918
I start with what the audience should feel.

00:00:12.918 --> 00:00:16.866
I give the agent thirty seconds, a place, and a question about scale.

00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:20.814
I ask for two ways to tell it before anyone builds the shot.

00:00:21.613 --> 00:00:24.484
The first pitch uses a seam between photographs.

00:00:24.484 --> 00:00:26.996
The old summit gives way to sky.

00:00:26.996 --> 00:00:32.380
The second keeps us beside a walker, then pulls back until the person almost disappears.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.815
I choose the walker.

00:00:33.815 --> 00:00:37.763
A familiar body gives us a measure for an unfamiliar landscape.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.928
Now the choice has a practical consequence.

00:00:40.928 --> 00:00:43.669
We need the walls of this particular crater.

00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:48.808
Watch the agent find the public terrain tile, run the download, and check the file.

00:00:48.808 --> 00:00:51.892
The grid on the right comes from that tile.

00:00:51.892 --> 00:00:53.948
Its rows run north to south.

00:00:53.948 --> 00:00:55.661
Each sample carries an elevation.

00:00:56.447 --> 00:01:02.346
For the first cut, I give the agent an editable terrain renderer and an original stereo score.

00:01:02.346 --> 00:01:04.428
My direction is about the shot:

00:01:04.428 --> 00:01:09.979
begin six metres behind the walker, leave through the northern breach, and finish five kilometres away.

00:01:09.979 --> 00:01:13.449
The agent writes the camera path and builds the scene.

00:01:14.238 --> 00:01:15.496
Hereâ€™s the first cut.

00:01:15.496 --> 00:01:17.383
Keep your eye on the coat.

00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:19.585
By thirteen seconds itâ€™s already a dot.

00:01:19.585 --> 00:01:23.988
The camera is covering distance, but we lose our sense of whoâ€™s standing there.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.926 position:74% size:48% align:center
I mark the stretch where I lose the walker.

00:01:27.926 --> 00:01:31.072 position:74% size:48% align:center
My note gives the agent something it can test:

00:01:31.072 --> 00:01:35.266 position:74% size:48% align:center
stay within twelve metres through second thirteen, then climb on the swell.

00:01:35.266 --> 00:01:38.412 position:74% size:48% align:center
The mark carries that exact interval into the conversation.

00:01:39.197 --> 00:01:42.723 line:12% size:67% align:center
The agent measures the score and changes the code.

00:01:42.723 --> 00:01:46.250 line:12% size:67% align:center
This close range holds the camera near the walker.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:51.736 line:12% size:67% align:center
Starting at thirteen seconds, the next line blends back into the original five-kilometre pullback.

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:54.087 line:12% size:67% align:center
The same terrain now reads differently.

00:01:54.863 --> 00:01:57.072
Both cuts start here at eight seconds.

00:01:57.072 --> 00:01:58.965
Watch the coat on the right.

00:01:58.965 --> 00:02:02.120
At thirteen seconds you can still see a person walking.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:06.853
When the camera finally leaves, you remember the size of the body against the wall.

00:02:07.613 --> 00:02:10.368
Studio renders the scene with the stereo score.

00:02:10.368 --> 00:02:12.433
Here is the complete thirty-second film.

00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:17.597
Listen for the footfalls to give way to wind as the rim comes into view.

00:02:18.083 --> 00:02:22.583
[Footsteps on gravel. Low wind and music.]

00:02:36.083 --> 00:02:42.083
[The score swells as the camera rises.]
